# How Do You Really Detect OS Coupling in Modern Applications?

When architects talk about **OS coupling**, the conversation often starts with simple questions:

> "Does this application run on Linux?"

or:

> "Is the application running on RHEL?"

Those questions are too simplistic.

Almost every modern enterprise application runs on some operating system. A Java application running inside a Linux container does not automatically mean the application is tightly coupled to Linux.

The more important architectural question is:

> **If we upgrade, replace, or change the underlying operating system, what part of the application will break or require modification?**

That is the real definition of OS coupling.

OS coupling is fundamentally a **change-impact problem**. Coupling becomes important when a change in one component can force changes elsewhere.

And in modern enterprises, identifying it is becoming increasingly difficult because an "application" is no longer a single repository.

It may consist of:

* Microservices
* Monoliths
* Containers
* Serverless functions
* Batch jobs
* Shared libraries
* Data pipelines
* Integration services
* Infrastructure-as-Code
* Deployment repositories
* CI/CD pipelines
* Vendor/COTS components
* Legacy applications
* Desktop or thick-client applications
* Hybrid combinations of all of the above

So how do we actually detect OS coupling?

---

# The Problem With Repository-Level Scanning

Consider an application called **Payments Platform**.

It might contain:

```text
Payments Platform
│
├── payment-api
├── payment-engine
├── payment-ui
├── payment-batch
├── payment-scheduler
├── payment-shared-library
├── payment-deployment
├── payment-infrastructure
└── payment-data-pipeline
```

These may be ten different repositories owned by multiple teams.

Now imagine you scan only:

```text
payment-api
```

You find:

```text
Java 21
Spring Boot
Docker
```

Everything looks portable.

You conclude:

> OS coupling = Low

But another repository might contain:

```text
payment-batch
    └── systemctl
    └── shell scripts
    └── /etc/payment
```

And another:

```text
payment-shared-library
    └── libssl.so.1.1
```

And another:

```text
payment-infrastructure
    └── RHEL 8 AMI
    └── yum install
```

And another:

```text
payment-deployment
    └── systemd service
```

Now the actual application has significant OS coupling.

This leads to an important principle:

> **OS coupling cannot reliably be assessed at repository level. It must be assessed at application-component level and then aggregated across repositories, deployment units, and infrastructure.**

---

# Step 1: Define the Application Boundary

Before scanning code, establish:

> **What constitutes the application?**

The architecture platform should create an **Application Inventory**.

For example:

| Component      | Repository        | Runtime      | Deployment     | Infrastructure |
| -------------- | ----------------- | ------------ | -------------- | -------------- |
| Payment API    | payment-api       | Java 21      | Container      | ECS            |
| Payment Engine | payment-engine    | Python 3.12  | Container      | ECS            |
| Payment Batch  | payment-batch     | Python/Shell | VM             | RHEL           |
| Payment UI     | payment-ui        | Node.js      | Static hosting | Managed        |
| Scheduler      | payment-scheduler | Java         | Container      | Kubernetes     |
| Shared Library | payment-common    | C++          | Package        | Native         |
| Infrastructure | payment-infra     | Terraform    | IaC            | AWS            |
| Deployment     | payment-deploy    | Shell/YAML   | CI/CD          | Jenkins        |

This becomes the foundation of the assessment.

---

# Step 2: Don't Assume One Application Has One Architecture Style

A modern enterprise application may be:

### Monolithic

```text
Application
     ↓
VM
     ↓
RHEL
```

### Microservices

```text
Application
   │
   ├── Service A
   ├── Service B
   ├── Service C
   └── Service D
```

### Containerized

```text
Application
     ↓
Container
     ↓
Container Runtime
     ↓
Host OS
```

### Serverless

```text
Application
     ↓
Lambda / Function
     ↓
Managed Runtime
```

### Batch

```text
Scheduler
    ↓
Shell/Python/Java
    ↓
VM
    ↓
OS
```

### Data Pipeline

```text
Pipeline
   ↓
Spark/Python
   ↓
Runtime
   ↓
Managed Platform
```

### Legacy

```text
Application
   ↓
Application Server
   ↓
Specific OS
   ↓
Specific Hardware
```

### Hybrid

And increasingly:

```text
                   Application
                        │
       ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
       ↓                ↓                ↓
   Microservices       Batch          Serverless
       ↓                ↓                ↓
  Containers           VM            Managed Runtime
       ↓                ↓                ↓
     Linux            RHEL              Managed
```

The assessment engine must understand all of these.

---

# Step 3: Scan Seven Sources of Evidence

OS coupling is not only in source code.

I would inspect **seven evidence sources**.

## 1. Application Source Code

Search for direct OS interaction.

Examples:

```text
system()
exec()
subprocess()
Runtime.exec()
ProcessBuilder
shell=True
PowerShell
cmd.exe
```

Filesystem references:

```text
/etc
/var
/opt
/usr
/tmp

C:\Windows
C:\Program Files
```

OS APIs:

```text
Win32
Windows Registry
POSIX
Linux system calls
kernel APIs
```

These are strong indicators of OS coupling.

---

# 2. Dependency and Build Files

The application may not directly call the OS, but one of its dependencies might.

Inspect:

```text
pom.xml
build.gradle
package.json
requirements.txt
poetry.lock
go.mod
Cargo.toml
.csproj
```

Look for:

```text
JNI
native libraries
DLL
SO
glibc
OpenSSL
libstdc++
platform-specific packages
```

For example:

```text
Python
   ↓
Native package
   ↓
glibc
   ↓
Linux
```

The Python source code may look completely portable.

The binary dependency may not be.

Native dependencies are therefore an important source of hidden coupling.

---

# 3. Container Definitions

For containerized applications, inspect:

```text
Dockerfile
Containerfile
Helm
Kubernetes manifests
ECS task definitions
```

For example:

```dockerfile
FROM rhel:8

RUN yum install openssl
RUN yum install libaio

COPY start.sh /app/

ENTRYPOINT ["/bin/bash", "/app/start.sh"]
```

This provides several signals:

```text
RHEL 8
yum
OpenSSL
libaio
bash
```

But this is where architecture reasoning is important.

Simply seeing:

```text
FROM ubuntu:24.04
```

does **not** mean high OS coupling.

A container necessarily has a userspace environment.

The question is:

> **Does the application depend on OS-specific behavior beyond the normal container/runtime boundary?**

Containers improve portability, but they do not provide absolute OS independence. The container image still has compatibility relationships with its host kernel and architecture.

---

# 4. Infrastructure-as-Code

This is one of the most overlooked areas.

Scan:

```text
Terraform
CloudFormation
CDK
Ansible
Packer
Chef
Puppet
AMI definitions
```

For example:

```terraform
ami = "rhel-8-payment"
```

or:

```text
packages:
  - openssl-1.1
  - libaio
```

The application repository might have no evidence of OS coupling.

But the infrastructure repository tells a different story.

This is why **application architecture assessment cannot be limited to application source code**.

---

# 5. Deployment Scripts

Look for:

```text
systemctl
service
chkconfig
cron
crontab
/etc/init.d
PowerShell
Windows Service
registry
```

For example:

```bash
systemctl restart payment-service
```

This indicates that the deployment lifecycle depends on systemd.

However, there is an important distinction:

> This may be **deployment OS coupling**, rather than **application-runtime OS coupling**.

Your assessment should preserve that distinction.

---

# 6. CI/CD Pipelines

Inspect:

```text
Jenkins
GitHub Actions
GitLab CI
Azure DevOps
Bamboo
```

Example:

```yaml
steps:
  - run: yum install gcc
  - run: ./build.sh
  - run: systemctl restart application
```

You may discover that the application can theoretically run anywhere, but the **build and deployment process cannot**.

That still matters.

A production OS upgrade can fail because the deployment pipeline depends on the old OS.

---

# 7. Runtime and Infrastructure Metadata

Finally, determine where the workload actually runs.

For example:

```text
Payment API
   ↓
Container
   ↓
ECS
   ↓
EC2
   ↓
Amazon Linux
```

Or:

```text
Payment Batch
   ↓
EC2
   ↓
RHEL 8
```

Or:

```text
Payment Function
   ↓
Lambda
   ↓
Managed Runtime
```

This allows the architecture engine to connect **code evidence with runtime reality**.

---

# OS Dependency Is Not the Same as OS Coupling

This distinction is critical.

Consider:

```text
Java application
      ↓
Linux
```

There is an OS dependency.

But it may have:

**OS coupling = LOW**

because the application can run unchanged across supported Linux distributions.

Now consider:

```text
Java application
      ↓
systemctl
      ↓
Linux systemd
```

Now:

**OS coupling = HIGH**

because the application or its operational lifecycle depends on an OS-specific mechanism.

---

# Another Example: Containers

Consider:

```text
Application
    ↓
Container
    ↓
Linux
```

This should not automatically receive a high OS-coupling score.

But consider:

```text
Application
    ↓
Container
    ↓
hostPath
    ↓
/var/lib/...
```

or:

```text
privileged: true
```

or:

```text
host network
```

Now the application is interacting much more closely with the host environment.

The coupling has increased.

---

# Another Example: Native Libraries

Consider:

```text
Application
      ↓
Native library
      ↓
libssl.so.1.1
      ↓
OS
```

An OS upgrade could change the availability or ABI compatibility of that library.

That is much stronger coupling than:

```text
Java
   ↓
JVM
   ↓
Linux
```

This is why the assessment must understand **dependency semantics**, not simply count OS-related strings.

---

# Build an Evidence Graph

This is the model I would use for an AI architecture assessment platform.

```text
                     APPLICATION
                          │
                          ↓
                  COMPONENT INVENTORY
                          │
          ┌───────────────┼───────────────┐
          ↓               ↓               ↓
       Repo A           Repo B          Repo C
          │               │               │
          ↓               ↓               ↓
      Source         Dependencies      Dockerfile
          │               │               │
          └───────────────┼───────────────┘
                          ↓
                    EVIDENCE GRAPH
                          │
        ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
        ↓                 ↓                 ↓
    OS API          Native Library      OS Command
        │                 │                 │
        └─────────────────┼─────────────────┘
                          ↓
                   COUPLING ENGINE
                          ↓
                   RISK ASSESSMENT
```

Now every finding has traceability:

```text
Risk
 ↓
Finding
 ↓
Repository
 ↓
File
 ↓
Line
 ↓
Evidence
```

This is much more useful to an architect than an AI-generated statement with no evidence.

---

# Don't Ask the LLM to Detect Everything

This is particularly important if you're building an AI architecture assessment agent.

I would use:

```text
Static Analysis
       +
Dependency Analysis
       +
Container Analysis
       +
IaC Analysis
       +
CI/CD Analysis
       +
Runtime Metadata
       ↓
Evidence
       ↓
LLM
       ↓
Architectural Reasoning
```

The deterministic tools find the evidence.

The LLM interprets it.

For example:

### Deterministic scanner

Finds:

```text
systemctl
libssl.so.1.1
RHEL 8 AMI
/etc/payment/config
```

### AI reasoning

Concludes:

> The Payment Batch component has high OS coupling because its deployment depends on systemd, its runtime depends on a version-specific native OpenSSL library, and its infrastructure is pinned to RHEL 8. A RHEL 8 → RHEL 9 migration may therefore require changes to the deployment process and native dependency stack.

That is where AI adds value.

---

# A Practical OS Coupling Classification

I would define rules such as:

| Evidence                             | Coupling       |
| ------------------------------------ | -------------- |
| Application runs on Linux            | 🟢 Low         |
| Container has Linux base image       | 🟢 Low         |
| Standard JVM/.NET/Python runtime     | 🟢 Low         |
| OS-specific filesystem path          | 🟡 Medium      |
| Shell command dependency             | 🟡 Medium      |
| OS-specific package                  | 🟠 Medium/High |
| systemd / Windows Service dependency | 🔴 High        |
| Version-specific native library      | 🔴 High        |
| OS-specific API                      | 🔴 High        |
| Kernel module/API dependency         | 🔴 Critical    |
| Direct host filesystem access        | 🔴 High        |
| Privileged container                 | 🔴 High        |

These should be **rules**, not LLM guesses.

---

# Then Ask the Most Important Question

For every finding, your assessment engine should ask:

> **"If the underlying OS is upgraded or replaced, can this component continue to operate without changing application code, dependencies, configuration, build, or deployment?"**

If the answer is:

### Yes

```text
OS Coupling = Low
```

### Possibly, but requires testing/configuration

```text
OS Coupling = Medium
```

### Application/deployment changes required

```text
OS Coupling = High
```

### Fundamental redesign or recompilation required

```text
OS Coupling = Critical
```

This is much more meaningful than simply counting OS references.

---

# Application-Level Assessment

Now imagine the application has:

```text
Payment API          → LOW
Payment Engine       → LOW
Payment Batch        → HIGH
Shared Library       → CRITICAL
Deployment           → HIGH
Infrastructure       → HIGH
```

Don't calculate a simple average.

A critical dependency in a business-critical shared library may dominate the application-level risk.

Instead evaluate:

```text
Severity
+
Business Criticality
+
Blast Radius
+
Production Usage
+
Replaceability
+
Upgrade Impact
+
Compatibility Testing
```

The final result might be:

```text
PAYMENTS PLATFORM

OS COUPLING
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

72 / 100
HIGH
```

With:

```text
Top Findings

1. RHEL 8 dependency in batch workload
2. Version-specific OpenSSL native library
3. systemd-based deployment
4. OS-specific deployment scripts
5. No evidence of target OS compatibility testing
```

---

# The Architecture Target State

The objective is not to eliminate all OS dependencies.

That is neither realistic nor necessary.

The goal is to **control where the dependency exists**.

Instead of:

```text
Application
     ↓
OS-specific implementation
     ↓
Operating System
```

move toward:

```text
                 APPLICATION
                      │
                      ↓
              PORT / INTERFACE
                      │
                      ↓
             ADAPTER / RUNTIME
                      │
                      ↓
              INFRASTRUCTURE
                      │
                      ↓
                 OS / CLOUD
```

This is essentially the same architectural principle used in portable software design: keep platform-specific functionality isolated behind defined boundaries rather than spreading it throughout application logic.

---

# The Bigger Opportunity: Application Portability

I would actually go one step further.

Don't make **OS Coupling** a standalone architecture metric.

Make it part of:

# Application Portability & Infrastructure Coupling

```text
Application Portability
│
├── OS Coupling
├── Runtime Coupling
├── Database Coupling
├── Cloud Coupling
├── Infrastructure Coupling
├── Vendor Coupling
└── Platform Coupling
```

For example:

```text
APPLICATION PORTABILITY

OS Coupling                  72 🔴
Runtime Coupling             41 🟡
Database Coupling            65 🟠
Cloud Coupling               38 🟢
Infrastructure Coupling      54 🟡
Vendor Coupling              47 🟡

Overall Portability          53 🟠
```

Now an architect can answer a much more valuable question:

> **"How difficult would it be to change the underlying technology or infrastructure without breaking this application?"**

That is a much broader and more strategic architecture question than simply:

> "Does this application depend on Linux?"

---

# Final Thought

Modern applications are no longer single codebases.

They are ecosystems of:

**Repositories → Components → Dependencies → Runtimes → Containers → Infrastructure → Operating Systems.**

Therefore, OS coupling cannot be discovered by scanning a single repository.

It requires a **multi-layer evidence model**.

The architecture assessment should:

```text
Discover
   ↓
Classify
   ↓
Map
   ↓
Scan
   ↓
Correlate
   ↓
Reason
   ↓
Assess
   ↓
Recommend
```

And the most important question should always remain:

> **"What will break if the OS changes?"**

If your architecture agent can answer that question with **evidence, impact, blast radius, and remediation**, then you have moved beyond a simple compliance checker into a genuine **AI-powered architecture risk assessment capability**.
